description:
Define the SieveBackend trait in crate-jmap-mail-server/src/sieve.rs.

WHAT: SieveBackend is the opt-in trait storage backends implement to support the sieve feature. Separate from MailBackend.

ALGORITHM:
1. Create crate-jmap-mail-server/src/sieve.rs (no cfg attributes in the file itself — gates are in lib.rs).
2. Define:
   pub trait SieveBackend: Send + Sync {
     type Error: std::error::Error + Send + Sync + 'static;

     // Fetch raw bytes for a blob (used by validate handler to read script content)
     fn get_sieve_blob(&self, account_id: &Id, blob_id: &Id)
       -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<Vec<u8>>, Self::Error>> + Send;

     // Validate Sieve script content. Returns Ok(None) if valid; Ok(Some(description)) if invalid
     // (description SHOULD include line number per spec §2.6). Returns Err for storage failures.
     fn validate_sieve_script(&self, account_id: &Id, blob_id: &Id)
       -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<String>, Self::Error>> + Send;
   }

3. Define result structs used by the set handler:
   - No bespoke result structs needed: the set handler uses MailBackend::create_object/update_object/
     destroy_object (all via the generic trait) and assembles created/updated/notCreated/etc. maps itself.

DEFENSIVE RULES:
- Trait must be Send + Sync
- Associated type Error: std::error::Error + Send + Sync + 'static (same pattern as MdnBackend)
- All futures must be + Send
- No .unwrap() in library code
- Return Ok(None) from get_sieve_blob for missing blob (not an error); Err only for storage failures

AGENT TEAM: Single implementer

TESTS: cargo check -p jmap-mail-server --features sieve passes; this bead is structural — full tests come in integration test bead

design:
Trait definition (behind cfg(feature=sieve)):

pub trait SieveBackend: Send + Sync {
    async fn get_sieve_scripts(&self, account_id: &Id, ids: Option<&[Id]>) -> Result<GetResult<SieveScript>, BackendChangesError>;
    async fn set_sieve_scripts(&self, account_id: &Id, req: SieveSetRequest) -> Result<SetResult<SieveScript>, BackendSetError>;
    async fn query_sieve_scripts(&self, account_id: &Id, filter: Option<SieveScriptFilter>, sort: Option<Vec<Comparator<SieveScriptSortProperty>>>, position: i64, limit: Option<u64>, anchor: Option<&Id>, anchor_offset: i64) -> Result<QueryResult, BackendChangesError>;
    async fn validate_sieve_script(&self, account_id: &Id, blob_id: &Id) -> Result<Option<SieveSetError>, BackendChangesError>;
}

Note on set_sieve_scripts: the backend is responsible for the activation state machine (at-most-one-active invariant). on_success_activate_script and on_success_deactivate_script are forwarded to the backend; the handler applies them only after all creates/updates/destroys succeed. The backend returns updated isActive entries in SetResult.

Note on validate: backend retrieves blob content, parses Sieve grammar, returns None if valid or SieveSetError { type: InvalidSieve, description: Some(line_number_message) } if invalid.
